    {
      "title": "Gauff and Pegula reach historic all-American Cincinnati final in 56 years",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/sports/other-sports-news/gauff-and-pegula-reach-historic-all-american-cincinnati-final-in-56-years-126082300117_1.html",
      "description": "Jessica Pegula beat defending champion Iga Swiatek and Coco Gauff defeated Sara Bejlek on Saturday to set up the first all-American women's final at the Cincinnati Open in 56 years.\nPegula rallied from down a break in the third set to get past Swiatek, 7-5, 4-6, 6-4, for her tour-leading 45th win this year. It was the 300th tour-level victory of Pegula's career, and it snapped Swiatek's 10-match winning streak.\nPegula's best finish in Cincinnati came in 2024, when she lost to Aryna Sabalenka in the final.\nGauff, the 2023 champion, beat Bejlek, 6-4, 6-1. The 20-year-old Czech upset top seed Aryna Sabalenka and Madison Keys en route to the semis.\nThe last time two American women played in the Cincinnati final was in 1970, when Rosie Casals beat Nancy Richey.\n\"I'm always rooting for American tennis,\" Pegula said. \"I like to play someone that I get along with and have a lot of respect for, like Coco. I didn't realize it was so long since we've had a double American final. The crowd ca",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-23T05:46:30"
    }
